Recent analyses of lepton flavor universality (LFU) violating decays have suggested that new physics beyond the Standard Model might exist, potentially influencing multiple areas. To gain insights into these potential effects, we have explored their implications on the decay $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ within the $U_1$ leptoquark model. Although this model can readily explain LFU violations, analyzing the decay $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ alone isn't sufficient to fully constrain it. To overcome this limitation, we performed both theoretical and experimental assessments of relevant quantities, ultimately showing that combining LFU violating data with $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ analyses can successfully constrain the $U_1$ leptoquark model. Furthermore, if $b 	o c$ data were perfect, they would be sufficient to fully constrain the $U_1$ leptoquark model, whereas current data yield only loose constraints. As such, we highlight the significance of future experiments, specifically LHCb and potentially Belle II, to thoroughly analyze $ ar{
u}$ and provide more detailed analyses of relevant quantities to further evaluate the lepton flavor universality violating structures.
